{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013GZV5NYbXP26dZ3fzjLPgp","type":"tool_result","content":"VENUE.md\nanalysis\nbuild_figures.py\nclaims_grounding.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\ndraft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.json\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.md\nfigure_manifest.json\nfigures\nglobal_memory\nglobal_memory_recent.json\nglobal_memory_summary.md\nmemory.md\npaper.bst\npaper.pdf\npaper.sty\npaper.tex\npaper_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\npaper_data.json\npaper_diagnostics\npaper_diagnostics_20261027_182218_8711.log\npaper_events_20261027_182218.jsonl\npaper_prep_20261027_182218.json\npaper_prompt.md\npaper_strategy.json\npaper_strategy.md\npaper_strategy_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\npaper_strategy_prompt.md\npaper_strategy_resolution.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan.json\nreference_query_plan.md\nreference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\nreference_retrieval.log\nreferences.bib\nreferences_provenance.json\nresults\nscientific-evidence\nsource_evidence\nstrategy_validation_feedback.json\nstrategy_validation_feedback.md\ntask\n# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n**Summary:** The draft (`paper.tex`) cites only five classical cover-time sources. Two citation gaps are worth fixing with a small, targeted retrieval pass:\n\n1. One comparator has no citation, and another is credited to the wrong paper.\n2. The positioning and discussion text refers to family-specific cover-time asymptotics without citing any.\n\nNeither fix needs a rewrite of the paper's story.\n\n## Current coverage\n\nThe draft's current coverage was checked against `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json`:\n\n- **Aleliunas et al. 1979.** Cited for the 2m(n−1) bound. Adequate.\n- **Chandra et al. 1989.** Cited for the commute-time and effective-resistance link. Adequate.\n- **Lovász survey.** Cited for the closed forms and general background. Adequate.\n- **Feige 1995, \"A Tight *Lower* Bound on the Cover Time\".** Cited both for the n ln n lower bound and for the (4/27)n³ *upper* bound. The upper-bound attribution is wrong; see Target 1.\n- **Retrieval failures.** The original retrieval hit HTTP 429 on the queries for the 2m(n−1) bound and Feige's upper bound. The query for the Matthews bound returned no candidates. This explains the gaps below.\n\nAll quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and `analysis/` through `@evidence` tags. None of them needs a literature citation.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. `related-work-comparators`: Background and Related Work (also Introduction, Method §Compared predictors, Discussion). Priority **high**. Intent: comparator.\n\n**Anchors:**\n- \"Matthews' method bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$.\"\n- \"Feige's upper bound $\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case…\"\n\n**Why this is high priority:**\n- **Matthews has no citation.** The Matthews-type reference is a row in the main results table (Table 2, error 0.605), and the text discusses it as \"a more demanding comparison\". It is never cited, either in Background or in the Compared predictors list.\n- **The cubic bound is credited to the wrong paper.** The best bound min{2m(n−1), (4/27)n³} is the primary comparator. Its cubic term is credited to Feige1995ATL, which is the lower-bound paper. The upper bound comes from Feige's separate tight-upper-bound paper. The same misattribution recurs in the Introduction, the Design rationale and the Discussion, where the lollipop attains (4/27)n³.\n- **Reviewers will check.** Reviewers at a venue that rewards honest baselines will check where each comparator comes from.\n\n**Search topics:**\n- Matthews, \"Covering problems for Markov chains\"\n- Feige, \"A tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\"\n- Optional textbook anchor for the Matthews method and hitting-time computation: Levin–Peres–Wilmer, *Markov Chains and Mixing Times*\n\n### 2. `positioning-family-asymptotics`: Background and Related Work (Positioning), supporting the Discussion. Priority **medium**. Intent: benchmark_context.\n\n**Anchor:** \"it derives bounds that hold for all graphs, or asymptotics for specific families\"\n\n**Why it matters:**\n- **The Positioning paragraph leaves a gap.** It points to family-specific asymptotic work but cites none of it.\n- **The Discussion depends on that work without citing it:**\n  - It says random 3-regular and dense Erdős–Rényi graphs lie in the near-n ln n regime, which is where the gains are largest.\n  - It suggests that grid cover times are not a single power law at this scale, which is offered as the reason grids have high error.\n- **One to three citations would close the gap.** Citations for random-regular, random-graph and 2D-torus cover-time asymptotics would ground both points and connect the chosen test families to known results. The narrative stays unchanged.\n\n**Search topics:**\n- Cooper–Frieze, cover time of random regular graphs\n- Cooper–Frieze, cover time of sparse random graphs (Erdős–Rényi)\n- Dembo–Peres–Rosen–Zeitouni, cover times for random walks in two dimensions (torus)\n\n## Deliberately not targeted\n\n- **Quantitative results** (Tables 2–3, per-family numbers, controls). These are grounded in evaluator output.\n- **Simulator closed forms** (C₁₂, K₁₀, P₁₀). These are already covered by the Lovász survey.\n- **The \"we are not aware of\" positioning sentence.** No search on ML or GNN prediction of graph properties is requested. The run used neither. The reference plan also forbids queries phrased as the research question, because they could surface the original paper. Adding that literature would mean reshaping the paper's framing.\n- **The unevaluated physics-mixture variant.** There is no execution evidence for it, so no literature is needed.\n\n## Non-retrieval hygiene note (for the later rewrite stage)\n\n`references.bib` lists the Lovász survey twice: `Asz1993RandomWO` (with a garbled author name) and `Lovász2001RandomWO`. Only the 2001 key is cited in the draft. The duplicate needs no retrieval, but the later rewrite stage should drop it.\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"The draft cites only five classical cover-time sources. Two gaps matter: the Matthews comparator in the main table has no citation and the (4/27)n^3 upper bound is credited to Feige's lower-bound paper; the positioning and discussion text also refers to family-specific cover-time asymptotics without citing any.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"Matthews hitting-time cover-time bound\",\n    \"Feige tight upper bound on cover time\",\n    \"family-specific cover-time asymptotics (random regular, Erdos-Renyi, 2D grid/torus)\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"related-work-comparators\",\n      \"section\": \"Background and Related Work\",\n      \"priority\": \"high\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"comparator\",\n      \"reason\": \"The Matthews-type reference (max hitting time x H_{n-1}) is a comparator in Table 2 and is described in Background (Graph-aware characterizations) and Method (Compared predictors) with no citation. The (4/27)n^3 'Feige upper bound', the paper's primary best-bound comparator, is cited only to Feige1995ATL, whose title is 'A Tight Lower Bound on the Cover Time'. The same misattribution appears in the Introduction, Background, Design rationale and Discussion. A reviewer checking comparator provenance would catch both problems.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"Matthews' method bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$. / Feige's upper bound $\\\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Matthews covering problems for Markov chains\",\n        \"Feige a tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\",\n        \"Levin Peres Wilmer Markov chains and mixing times cover time Matthews method\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"positioning-family-asymptotics\",\n      \"section\": \"Background and Related Work\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"reason\": \"The Positioning paragraph says prior work derives 'asymptotics for specific families' but cites none. The Discussion then relies on such results without citations: random 3-regular and dense Erdos-Renyi graphs sit in the near-n ln n regime, and grid cover time may not follow a single power law. One to three citations for random-regular, random-graph and 2D-torus cover-time asymptotics would ground these statements and the choice of evaluated families. The story does not need to change.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"it derives bounds that hold for all graphs, or asymptotics for specific families\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Cooper Frieze cover time of random regular graphs\",\n        \"Cooper Frieze cover time of sparse random graphs Erdos-Renyi giant component\",\n        \"Dembo Peres Rosen Zeitouni cover times for Brownian motion and random walks in two dimensions\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Empirical prediction of the expected cover time of simple random walks on small connected graphs (n <= 30) from a few cheap graph statistics (n, m, degrees, diameter), compared against classical worst-case cover-time bounds, with per-family error and Monte Carlo uncertainty.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"log-linear ridge regression of Monte Carlo cover-time estimates on log graph statistics (n, m, mean/max/min degree, diameter, log n * log diameter)\",\n    \"Monte Carlo estimation of uniform-start expected cover time with standard errors\",\n    \"exact maximum hitting time computation for the Matthews reference bound\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Aleliunas-Karp-Lipton-Lovasz-Rackoff bound 2m(n-1)\",\n    \"Feige tight upper bound (4/27) n^3\",\n    \"Matthews bound: max hitting time times harmonic number\",\n    \"electrical-network / commute-time characterisation of cover time\",\n    \"constant and OLS log-linear reference predictors\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"programmatically generated graph families: path, cycle, star, complete, 2D grid, binary tree, lollipop, random tree, Erdos-Renyi, random 3-regular (5 <= n <= 30)\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"from worst-case analytic bounds to a data-fitted statistics-only log-linear predictor (the final model beats the best bound in every family; lollipop gain CI touches zero)\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"classical bounds are loosest on dense / expander-like families (complete, Erdos-Renyi, random 3-regular), where cover time scales like n log n\",\n    \"smallest gains on the lollipop family, the extremal n^3 cover/hitting-time construction\",\n    \"grid and complete/ER families have the largest residual log error of the fitted predictor\",\n    \"feature permutation destroys accuracy, so the statistics carry real signal\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\"query\": \"random walks universal traversal sequences complexity of maze problems\", \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\", \"rationale\": \"Source of the 2m(n-1) cover-time bound used as the evaluator's AKLLR comparator; listed starter literature.\"},\n    {\"query\": \"tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\", \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\", \"rationale\": \"Feige's (4/27)n^3 bound is the second comparator in best_bound; listed starter literature.\"},\n    {\"query\": \"Matthews covering problems for Markov chains hitting time bound\", \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\", \"rationale\": \"The matthews_ref comparator is max hitting time times H_{n-1}; needs the original Matthews citation.\"},\n    {\"query\": \"Lovasz random walks on graphs survey\", \"category\": \"core_method\", \"rationale\": \"Standard survey for hitting, commute and cover times; listed starter literature and background for the definitions used.\"},\n    {\"query\": \"electrical resistance of a graph captures its commute and cover times\", \"category\": \"core_method\", \"rationale\": \"Explains why m, degree and diameter statistics should predict cover time (cover time ~ m * effective resistance up to log n), motivating the feature set.\"},\n    {\"query\": \"Feige tight lower bound cover time n log n\", \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\", \"rationale\": \"The n log n regime of complete, ER and random regular graphs is where bounds are loosest (largest gains); the lower bound literature frames this.\"},\n    {\"query\": \"cover time of random regular graphs and sparse random graphs Cooper Frieze\", \"category\": \"benchmark_context\", \"rationale\": \"Random 3-regular and Erdos-Renyi families are test families; asymptotic cover-time results contextualise predictor behaviour on them.\"},\n    {\"query\": \"cover times for Brownian motion and random walks in two dimensions torus\", \"category\": \"benchmark_context\", \"rationale\": \"Grid family has one of the largest residual errors; the 2D cover-time asymptotics (n log^2 n) explain the regime mismatch.\"},\n    {\"query\": \"maximum hitting time for random walks on graphs lollipop\", \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\", \"rationale\": \"Lollipop is the extremal n^3 construction and the family where gains over bounds are smallest (CI includes zero).\"},\n    {\"query\": \"Markov chains and mixing times Levin Peres Wilmer\", \"category\": \"core_method\", \"rationale\": \"Standard textbook reference for cover time, Matthews method and hitting-time computations used in the evaluation.\"}\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"queries phrased as the research question itself (e.g. 'predict cover time from graph statistics small graphs regression') that could surface the forbidden original paper or pages citing it\",\n    \"generic machine learning / benchmarking / optimisation queries (e.g. 'regression model evaluation', 'cross-validation')\",\n    \"graph neural network or learned graph-embedding queries (not used in the run)\",\n    \"chronology or campaign-history queries\",\n    \"the unevaluated physics-mixture variant from memory (no execution evidence; final model is the ridge baseline)\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9371b55d-ffc6-42c4-ac6a-69f635e1e3cf","uuid":"a42a18ca-633d-4261-b548-1bf73bd9f540","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:43:17.140Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"VENUE.md\nanalysis\nbuild_figures.py\nclaims_grounding.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\ndraft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.json\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.md\nfigure_manifest.json\nfigures\nglobal_memory\nglobal_memory_recent.json\nglobal_memory_summary.md\nmemory.md\npaper.bst\npaper.pdf\npaper.sty\npaper.tex\npaper_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\npaper_data.json\npaper_diagnostics\npaper_diagnostics_20261027_182218_8711.log\npaper_events_20261027_182218.jsonl\npaper_prep_20261027_182218.json\npaper_prompt.md\npaper_strategy.json\npaper_strategy.md\npaper_strategy_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\npaper_strategy_prompt.md\npaper_strategy_resolution.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan.json\nreference_query_plan.md\nreference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\nreferen