{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTEaYEqDgNStyfW8YF4c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012KzYpHrNGnxU33YBiMkfxN","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; cat > postdraft_reference_query_plan.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Post-draft citation repair for an empirical study that predicts the expected cover time of simple random walks on small connected graphs (5 <= n <= 30) from cheap graph statistics and compares the predictor with classical cover-time bounds. Two gaps are in scope: (1) comparator provenance, since the Matthews-type reference has no citation and the (4/27)n^3 upper bound is cited to Feige's lower-bound paper; (2) the family-specific cover-time asymptotics named in Background > Positioning and relied on in the Discussion.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Matthews method: cover time <= max hitting time x H_{n-1}, the bound behind the Matthews-type reference (Background 'Graph-aware characterizations', Method 'Compared predictors', Table 2)\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Feige tight upper bound (4/27)n^3 + o(n^3), the cubic term of the primary best-bound comparator (currently misattributed to Feige1995ATL, the lower-bound paper)\",\n    \"Matthews hitting-time cover-time bound (Table 2 row, error 0.605; currently uncited)\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"cover-time asymptotics for evaluated test families: random 3-regular graphs, Erdos-Renyi graphs above the connectivity threshold, 2D grids/tori (Positioning paragraph and Discussion)\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"near-n ln n regime of random regular and dense Erdos-Renyi graphs, where gains over the bounds are largest (Discussion)\",\n    \"grid cover time is not a single power law at this scale (n log^2 n asymptotics), offered as the reason grids have high residual error (Discussion)\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Feige A tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Gap target related-work-comparators (high). The (4/27)n^3 term of the primary best-bound comparator is cited to Feige1995ATL ('A Tight Lower Bound...') in the Introduction, Background, Design rationale and Discussion (lollipop attains (4/27)n^3). The separate upper-bound paper is needed to fix the attribution. The first-pass query for it hit HTTP 429, so this uses the exact title to avoid matching the lower-bound paper already in the bib.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Matthews Covering problems for Markov chains Annals of Probability\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Gap target related-work-comparators (high). The Matthews-type reference (max hitting time x H_{n-1}) is a Table 2 comparator, described as 'a more demanding comparison' and listed under Compared predictors, but it has no citation. The first-pass query returned zero candidates, so this uses the exact title plus venue.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Levin Peres Wilmer Markov Chains and Mixing Times cover time Matthews method\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Optional textbook anchor from the gap memo for the Matthews method and for computing hitting times by linear solve (Method, Matthews-type reference). This is also a fallback if the 1988 Matthews article is not indexed. Add at most one textbook citation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Cooper Frieze The cover time of random regular graphs\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Gap target positioning-family-asymptotics (medium). The Positioning sentence 'asymptotics for specific families' has no citation, and the Discussion places random 3-regular graphs in the near-n ln n regime where gains are largest. The asymptotic for random r-regular graphs ((r-1)/(r-2) n ln n) grounds both.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Cooper Frieze The cover time of sparse random graphs\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Gap target positioning-family-asymptotics (medium). The Discussion says Erdos-Renyi graphs, sampled from p ~ 2 ln n / n up to 0.5, sit near the n ln n regime and move between sparse and dense behaviour. The G(n, c ln n / n) cover-time asymptotic covers exactly this density range.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Dembo Peres Rosen Zeitouni Cover times for Brownian motion and random walks in two dimensions\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Gap target positioning-family-asymptotics (medium). The Discussion suggests that grid cover time is not a single power law in the statistics at this scale, as the explanation for high grid error. The 2D torus asymptotic (4/pi) n log^2 n grounds this. It also covers the remaining family named in the Positioning gap.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"queries phrased as the research question itself (e.g. 'predict cover time from graph statistics regression small graphs') that could surface the original paper or pages citing it\",\n    \"machine learning, GNN or learned graph-property prediction literature; the gap memo explicitly excludes support for the 'we are not aware of' sentence\",\n    \"re-searching sources already in references.bib: Aleliunas et al. 1979, Chandra et al. 1989, the Lovasz survey, Feige's lower-bound paper\",\n    \"generic regression, bootstrap or cross-validation methodology queries; the quantitative claims are grounded in paper_data.json and analysis/\",\n    \"the unevaluated physics-mixture variant from memory (no execution evidence)\",\n    \"a broad cover-time literature refresh beyond the two gap targets (e.g. lollipop hitting-time extremal results, cover-time algorithms, Gaussian free field connections)\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\nEOF\npython3 -c \"import json;d=j